# Database & Archives
*.sqlite
*.db

# Anchored with a leading slash, which is the whole point of this entry.
#
# A gitignore pattern with no slash in it matches at EVERY depth, so `exports/`
# meant "any directory called exports, anywhere". It was written for a top-level
# output directory and it silently swallowed `src/lib/report/exports/` --- four
# TypeScript modules that the Reports page imports. `git add -A` skipped them
# without a word, `git status` stayed clean because an ignored file is not
# untracked, and `tsc` passed here because the files were on disk. CI checked out
# a tree that did not contain them and failed with four TS2307s on modules that
# exist in every working copy.
#
# `archives/` had the identical flaw waiting for the first `src/.../archives/`.
/exports/
/archives/

# PyInstaller / Build Artifacts
engine/build/
engine/dist/
# PyInstaller's work directory lands wherever it was run from. `engine/build/`
# is the one the documented command produces; this catches a run from the repo
# root, which committed 92 MB of .pkg and .pyz before anyone noticed.
/build/
# Inno Setup's output. `npm run tauri build` writes its MSI and NSIS bundles
# under src-tauri/target/ (already ignored); ISCC.exe writes here instead, and
# the directory is empty until the first compile, so git never warned about it.
/dist-installer/
# Scratch dependency/licence listing. THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md is the kept record;
# this is the intermediate, and its own name says so.
.deps.tmp
# The compiled Python sidecar. Built by PyInstaller, never committed.
#
# This used to be `src-tauri/binaries/*.exe`, which was enough while the build
# was `--onefile`. It is now `--onedir`, so the output is a directory of ~1,400
# files (the interpreter, every dependency, and Tcl's timezone tables) — and the
# old pattern matched none of them. `wordlists/` lives beside it and *is*
# tracked: those are source data, synced into place by scripts/sync-wordlists.mjs.
src-tauri/binaries/ewac-engine/
src-tauri/binaries/*.exe
src-tauri/target/

# Build output for the localization regression tests
.test-build

# Generated by the PyInstaller spec on every build; describes the .exe, not the source.
engine/_build_info.py

# PDF regression baselines, written by `npm run test:export -- --write-baseline`.
#
# One per archive shape: a wireless survey and a LAN sweep draw different halves
# of the document, so a single pinned baseline checked about half the builder.
#
# Not tracked, deliberately -- and not for the size. The two of them come to
# 2.3 MB, where the three entries this replaced claimed ~17 MB for one of them. A
# baseline is survey data: it is the document produced from one archived scan on
# this rig, it changes whenever the report legitimately changes, and committing it
# would be committing somebody's radio measurements of somebody's street. The
# trade is stated in docs/TESTING.md -- the net catches a regression *between runs
# on one machine*, which is what a refactor needs, and a fresh clone records its
# own baselines on the first run rather than comparing against someone else's
# data.
scripts/baselines/

# Candidate reports kept when one differs from its baseline, so the difference can
# be investigated. Same reasoning as the baselines themselves.
scripts/candidate-*.pdf

# A PDF exported into the working directory.
#
# jsPDF's `save()` detects Node and writes with `fs` instead of triggering a
# download, so the component test that drives two real exports leaves its
# document in the repository root. Harmless in the application, where the same
# call hands the blob to the browser — but one of these was committed once, and a
# client's survey landing in version control is a worse accident than the clutter.
LOCKON_*.pdf
